# Libraries NEVER bundled into the URnetwork AppImage -- matched by glob
# against the library soname (basename) during build-appimage.sh's ldd walk.
# This is deliberately a data file: tuning happens here, not in code, and the
# first real Linux verification pass is expected to adjust it.
#
# Policy (documented decisions, APPIMAGE.md sections 4 and 11e):
#  - glibc family and the dynamic linker: never bundleable.
#  - Mesa / GL / DRM: the ecosystem excludelist rule. GTK4's GSK renderer is
#    GPU-based by default, and a bundled GL stack mismatching the host's
#    kernel/driver combination aborts inside gsk_gl_renderer_render.
#    Host-provide; GSK_RENDERER=cairo remains the user-side fallback.
#  - libstdc++/libgcc_s: excluded, host-provided. Bundling an OLDER libstdc++
#    is the documented trap: it wins via LD_LIBRARY_PATH and then the host's
#    Mesa/libLLVM cannot resolve GLIBCXX_* and every DRI driver breaks. The
#    build container must therefore BE the distro floor (Debian 12 /
#    Ubuntu 22.04) so the host's libstdc++ is always >= ours.
#  - fontconfig/freetype: excluded per the ecosystem excludelist (font cache
#    format mismatches); any GTK-capable host has them.
#  - X11/xcb/wayland client libs + libxkbcommon: excluded; any host that can
#    show a window has them, and protocol traffic is version-safe where ABI
#    pinning is not.
#  - dbus/systemd client libs: they talk to host daemons; host-provide.
#
# Everything NOT matched here gets bundled: gtk4, libadwaita, glib family
# (WITH its GIO modules -- never split), pango+harfbuzz (version-coupled),
# cairo, graphene, gdk-pixbuf + loaders, librsvg, epoxy, gettext runtime, ...

# dynamic linker + glibc
ld-linux*.so*
libc.so*
libm.so*
libdl.so*
libpthread.so*
librt.so*
libresolv.so*
libanl.so*
libutil.so*
libnsl.so*
libnss_*.so*
libmvec.so*
libthread_db.so*
libBrokenLocale.so*

# C++ runtime (host-provided; build on the floor distro)
libstdc++.so*
libgcc_s.so*

# GL / Mesa / DRM / vendor drivers
libGL.so*
